    {
      "id": "translation",
      "html": "<h2>English translation</h2><p class=\"translation-author\"><a href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5187-5163\">Marta Fogagnolo</a></p>  <p>The dwelling place of eternity in Christ. Makaria Euangelis, faithful in Christ, lived blamelessly for 20 years, 9 months, and 17 days. A most pure virgin, though she had been given in lawful marriage, she died one day before the Kalends of [...]. May your sleep be in peace.</p>  "
    },
    {
      "id": "commentary",
      "html": "<h2>commentary</h2>  <p>The funerary inscription opens with a reference to the tomb as the <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">οἶκος αἰῶνος ἐν (Χριστ)ῷ</span> (cf. Orsi 1893, 390) and the name the deceased, Makaria Euangelis: <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Μακαρία</span> in this case is a personal name and not an epithet (for the attestations of <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Μακαρία</span> in Sicily, see LGPN IIIA 286; <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Εὐαγγέλις</span> is not attested in the feminine form). The deceased, who died at a young age, had been given in marriage, according to Agnello's restoration of ll. 6–7, <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">ἁγνοτάτη νυνφευ|[θεῖσα ἐν γα]μῷ νομίμῳ</span>, in contrast to Orsi, who interpreted the passage as a reference to the Virgin Mary, restoring <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Νυμφυ|[τόκος</span>. Following the formula recording the day of death, the inscription concludes with the consolatory expression <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">εἰς εἰρήνην ἡ κοί|[μησίς σου</span> in ll. 9–10. This formula is frequently attested in Jewish inscriptions from Magna Graecia, including examples with the second-person singular possessive pronoun (cf. e.g., JIWE I 16, 46; II 39, 205), and is comparable to other expressions found in Sicilian inscriptions, such as <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">εἰς αἰῶνα μετὰ τῶν ἁγίων</span> (IG XIV 139, Syracuse) and <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">εἰς μνημόσυνον αἰώνων</span> (IG XIV 179, Syracuse). This expression, together with the reference to the Law at l. 7 (<span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">νομίμῳ</span>), identifies the inscription as Jewish rather than Christian, despite its repeated Christian professions (cf. Ferrua 1938, 73–74; Agnello 1953, 82).</p>  "
    },
